PLACES=[
# ---- Île d'Orléans ----
N('ancienne-eglise-saint-pierre-io',"Ancienne église de Saint-Pierre","Saint-Pierre-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','worship','standing',46.88889,-71.07347,'exact','view','walk','','1717–1719',
  "Built from 1717 to 1719 for one of the first parishes of the diocese of Quebec (erected 1678), this is the oldest church in Quebec still standing, according to the Quebec culture ministry's statement. It is a small fieldstone church in the shape of a Latin cross with a plain, steep-gabled front, a bull's-eye window over the door and a belfry on the ridge. Louis-Philippe Mariauchau d'Esgly, the first Canadian-born bishop, was its curé for 54 years and ran the diocese from here from 1784 to 1788. It was classified a historic monument in 1958.",
  "Outside: rubble-stone walls, cedar-shingle roof, the side door on the south wall with fluted pilasters, a yellow-brick sacristy on the apse, and the churchyard to the north behind a low stone wall with a wooden coping and an old wrought-iron steeple cross. Inside, by the Register: carved decoration designed by Thomas Baillairgé and made by André Paquet in the 1830s and 1840s (altarpiece with Corinthian columns, coffered window reveals, the churchwardens' pew), Pierre Émond's high altar (1795) and side altars (1800), a carved wooden sanctuary lamp of 1765, an enclosed spiral stair to the gallery, box pews with doors (1856) once kept warm with hot bricks, and the ornate stove pipe of 1862. The former presbytery (1851) stands below. Whether and when the public may go in: not checked.",
  "The oldest church left standing in Quebec, box pews and all.",
  "1249 chemin Royal, in the village. Owner and opening times not checked (no page of whoever runs it was found). Part of the Île-d'Orléans heritage site. Mapped lots beside the church on chemin Royal and avenue Monseigneur-D'Esgly (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Ancienne église de Saint-Pierre",7039),ARR],th=[46.88855,-71.07398],thn="Lot on chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),
N('eglise-sainte-famille-io',"Église de Sainte-Famille","Sainte-Famille-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','worship','standing',46.97381,-70.96216,'exact','view','walk','','1743–1747',
  "A stone parish church built from 1743 to 1747 and classified in 1980. Its front, modelled on the Jesuit church in Quebec City (demolished in 1804), is unusual for the French Regime: two square towers with belfries, a third belfry on the gable, a central door under a tall arched window, oculi and five niches with statues. The roof is painted vermilion.",
  "Inside, by the Register: a coffered false vault of 1812 by Louis-Basile David, wall decoration by Thomas Baillairgé (1821–1825), Corinthian altarpieces, two rear galleries and galleries in the transepts, three altars with carved tabernacles, a pulpit whose sounding board carries an angel with a trumpet, carved choir stalls and churchwardens' pew, gilding, and seven paintings set in the decoration, five by François Baillairgé and one by Frère Luc (1614–1685). A covered passage joins the sacristy to the transept. Interior hours not checked.",
  "A French Regime church with three belfries and a vermilion roof.",
  "On chemin Royal in the village, between the road and the river. Service times not found on a parish page. Lot on chemin Royal just southwest (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Église de Sainte-Famille",8860),ARR],th=[46.97338,-70.96286],thn="Lot on chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),
N('chapelle-procession-sainte-famille-io',"Chapelle de procession de Sainte-Famille","Sainte-Famille-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','worship','standing',46.9715,-70.9645,'site','view','walk','','early 19th century',
  "A small roughcast stone procession chapel, probably built in the first half of the 19th century, used as a resting altar on processions such as Corpus Christi. It stands on chemin Royal west of the church; its pair, on the other side of the village, is gone. Classified in 1981.",
  "A near-square nave ending in a round apse, a steep roof with a small bell-cote on the front, and a double door under a fan-shaped wooden tympanum. Inside, by the Register, a plank partition hides most of the apse, and the tabernacle, once in a side chapel of the parish church, is said to be by Gabriel Gosselin (1767). Interior not open as far as any source says.",
  "",
  "Roadside on chemin Royal; owner not checked. Park in the church lot about 250 m northeast and walk along the road.",
  [HP("Chapelle de procession de Sainte-Famille",11997),ARR],th=[46.97338,-70.96286],thn="Church lot on chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),
N('maison-drouin-io',"Maison Drouin","Sainte-Famille-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','landmark','standing',47.00196,-70.90005,'exact','open','drive','','1729–1736',
  "One of the oldest houses on the island: built in 1729–1730 for the Canac dit Marquis family and lengthened in 1734–1736; tree-ring dating shows the floor timbers were cut in the winter of 1729. The Canacs kept it until 1872, when it went to the blacksmith Élie Drouin and his descendants. The Fondation François-Lamy bought it in 1996 and opened it in 1997; it was classified in 2010.",
  "Rendered stone walls, a wood-shingle roof with a gabled dormer and a stone chimney. Inside, the finishes were conserved rather than repainted: a stone wall dividing the ground floor, the oldest room with its fireplace and bread oven, wide plank floors, a trapdoor to the cellar and a miller's stair to the attic. Electricity, put in about 1946, is a few bulbs and sockets. Guided tours, or a tablet tour of 27 short videos.",
  "A 1729 farmhouse left almost as the last family lived in it.",
  "2958 chemin Royal, near the Saint-François line. Owned and run by the Fondation François-Lamy (hours from its FAQ page); groups in spring by reservation. Admission fee. No lot is mapped in OpenStreetMap; parking not checked.",
  [W('Maison Drouin'),L('Fondation François-Lamy: Maison Drouin','https://www.fondationfrancoislamy.com/en/maison-drouin'),L('Fondation François-Lamy: questions and opening hours','https://www.fondationfrancoislamy.com/en/faq')],brick='no',hours='Mid-June through September: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. Fall: Saturdays and Sundays'),
N('eglise-saint-francois-io',"Église de Saint-François","Saint-François-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','worship','standing',47.00209,-70.81267,'exact','view','walk','','about 1734; rebuilt 1991–1992',
  "The stone church of the parish erected in 1679, built about 1734 by the master mason Thomas Allard. A fire in 1988 left only the masonry shell, and the church was rebuilt inside it in 1991 and 1992. The fire uncovered a graffito scratched in 1759 into an old layer of render by David Chapman, a gunner of HMS Neptune, flagship of the British fleet anchored off the island's east end, when the islanders had fled.",
  "The 18th-century stone walls: a single nave, a narrower choir with a round apse, an arched door and a spired belfry on the front. The church, presbytery, the old parish school and the cemetery with its calvary make one group. The interior is the 1990s rebuilding; whether the 1759 graffito can be seen was not checked.",
  "Rebuilt inside its 1734 walls after the 1988 fire, which revealed a British gunner's 1759 graffito.",
  "3497 chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap), in the village. Service times not found on a parish page. Lot on chemin Royal just south (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Église de Saint-François",13863),ARR],th=[47.00156,-70.81271],thn="Lot on chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),
N('ecole-fabrique-saint-francois-io',"École de fabrique de Saint-François","Saint-François-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','civic','standing',47.0025,-70.8125,'site','view','walk','','about 1830',
  "A parish (fabrique) school built about 1830 under Lower Canada's school laws of 1824 and 1829, which paid half the cost of a parish school building. Schools of this kind, forerunners of the rural one-room schools, served the Quebec countryside from 1829 to 1964. Classified in 1966 (the Register also gives 1957).",
  "A timber building of a storey and a half under a roof with flared eaves and three chimneys, set back from the road on an open lot near the church, presbytery and cemetery. Interior not checked.",
  "",
  "339 chemin Royal; owner not checked: look from the road. Park at the church lot on chemin Royal.",
  [HP("École de fabrique de Saint-François",13045),ARR],th=[47.00156,-70.81271],thn="Church lot on chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('eglise-saint-jean-io',"Église de Saint-Jean","Saint-Jean-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','worship','standing',46.92039,-70.88991,'exact','view','walk','','1734–1737',
  "Built from 1734 to 1737, one of the ten oldest churches left in Quebec. In 1852 Louis-Thomas Berlinguet and his son lengthened it at the front and gave it a neoclassical screen front with a pediment, a Palladian window and a big belfry, after the cathedral in Quebec City. Classified in 1957.",
  "Inside, by the Register: Louis-Basile David's pulpit, nave pilasters and churchwardens' pew with its canopy, one of the few such canopied pews kept (1810–1812); the false vault and altarpiece of 1831 by André Paquet to Thomas Baillairgé's design, which reuses parts of Jean Baillairgé's earlier altarpiece; and three paintings by Antoine Plamondon. Outside, the stone-walled cemetery, the presbytery and the church square with its Sacred Heart monument. Interior hours not checked.",
  "",
  "On rue de l'Église off chemin Royal. Service times not found on a parish page. Lot on rue de l'Église just south (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Église de Saint-Jean",10951),ARR],th=[46.92004,-70.88987],thn="Lot on rue de l'Église (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),
N('manoir-mauvide-genest-io',"Manoir Mauvide-Genest","Saint-Jean-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','landmark','standing',46.9141,-70.90267,'exact','open','walk','','1734',
  "A seigneurial manor house begun in 1734 for Jean Mauvide, grandson of Charles Genest, whose estate it was cut from; probably raised about 1738 and much enlarged before 1755. A chapel was added on the west gable in 1929. It is a national historic site (1993) for showing the seigneurial system of the French Regime, and was classified by Quebec in 1971.",
  "A big roughcast stone house of two and a half storeys under a steep hipped roof with two large chimneys and a nine-bay front, a summer pavilion and a shed of old date, and a stream through the grounds. Inside, by Parks Canada's statement, original finishes, 18th-century furnishings and fittings and the old room-to-room (en enfilade) layout survive; the museum furnishes all four floors as an 18th-century household and shows finds dug on the site in the early 2000s. Self-guided or guided visits, in French and English.",
  "A seigneur's stone manor of 1734, open to walk through.",
  "4818 chemin Royal, at the entry to the village. Run as a museum (hours and fees from its own page; the home page calls them seasonal hours and names no season). Two parking areas off chemin Royal, before and after the house: free for manor visitors. A park across the road runs down to the river. Kéroul-certified.",
  [HP('Mauvide-Genest Manor National Historic Site of Canada',12024),HP('Manoir Mauvide-Genest',13121),L('Manoir Mauvide-Genest: visits and fees','https://manoirmauvidegenest.com/visites-reservations/'),L('Manoir Mauvide-Genest: museum','https://manoirmauvidegenest.com/musee-et-visite-historique/')],th=[46.91464,-70.90176],thn='Manor lot on chemin Royal (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (season not stated). Guided tours at 11, 12, 1, 2, 3 and 4'),
N('chalouperie-godbout-io',"Chalouperie Godbout (Musée maritime de l'Île d'Orléans)","Saint-Laurent-de-l'Île-d'Orléans",'qc','industry','standing',46.85901,-71.01397,'site','open','walk','','about 1837–1840',
  "A rowboat shop built for François Godbout (the Register says about 1838 to 1840; the museum calls it the 1837 shop), worked by his family until David Godbout died in 1962. Islanders went from village to village and to the mainland in such boats; the bridge came only in 1935. In June 1989 the shop, stone foundations and all, was put on a barge and floated a few kilometres to the old Saint-Laurent shipyard (1908–1967), now the maritime museum.",
  "The timber shop with its big double doors, its old window pattern, a massive stone chimney and the outside opening of the steam box for bending planks, and inside more than 400 boatbuilding tools, classified as a collection. Outside: the shipyard grounds, the tugboat R.F. Grant and an audio tour of shipyard workers' memories.",
  "A boatbuilder's shop that sailed to its new home in 1989.",
  "120 chemin de la Chalouperie. Run by the museum (Fondation du Parc maritime de Saint-Laurent since 1995); admission fee, last guided tour 3 p.m., last admission 4 p.m. Free parking, toilets and picnic area.",
  [HP('Chalouperie Godbout',1690),L("Musée maritime de l'Île d'Orléans: plan your visit",'https://museemaritimeio.ca/en/plan-your-visit/'),L('Musée maritime: the Godbout rowboat shop','https://museemaritimeio.ca/en/godbout-rowboat-shop/'),L('Musée maritime: the Saint-Laurent shipyard','https://museemaritimeio.ca/en/the-shipyard-of-saint-laurent/')],th=[46.8594,-71.01395],thn='Museum lot on chemin de la Chalouperie (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',conf='mixed',hours='Early June to mid-October (June 1 to October 12 as the page gives it): daily 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
# ---- Côte-de-Beaupré ----
N('montmorency-1856-bridge-towers',"Montmorency Falls: towers of the 1856 suspension bridge",'Boischatel','qc','landmark','ruins',46.890706,-71.147952,'area','public','walk','','to 1856',
  "A wire-cable suspension bridge for wagons crossed the top of the 84-metre Montmorency Falls until April 30, 1856, when it failed and carried a wagon and its driver into the falls; several lives were lost, by the Quebec Gazette as Bridgemeister quotes it. People had crossed it in fear, and strands of one cable had already given way. The falls are a Quebec heritage site (1994) for their 17th-century farms, 18th-century farmhouses, 19th-century mills and power plants and two centuries of tourism.",
  "The towers (the Register says piers) of the 1856 bridge, still beside those of today's footbridge over the crest. The same park holds the remains of mills and the first hydroelectric works, the Manoir Montmorency on the site of Governor Haldimand's villa, St. Mary's Anglican chapel and a rebuilt 1759 British redoubt.",
  "A bridge that dropped a wagon into the falls in 1856: its towers still stand.",
  "Parc de la Chute-Montmorency, run by the Sépaq; parking and admission fees, hours not checked (sepaq.com refused the page tool). The pin is Bridgemeister's coordinate for the bridge, not checked against a picture of the towers. Mapped lots on the east (Boischatel) side; the main lots are by the manor on the west side.",
  [HP('Site historique de la Chute-Montmorency',4112),W('Montmorency Falls'),L('Bridgemeister: Montmorency Falls (1856)','https://m.bridgemeister.com/bridge.php?bid=1526')],th=[46.8929,-71.14414],thn='Lot off avenue Royale, Boischatel, east of the falls (seen on the aerial)',brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('manoir-de-charleville-boischatel',"Manoir de Charleville",'Boischatel','qc','landmark','standing',46.9036,-71.1211,'site','view','drive','','1660s',
  "One of the oldest houses on the Côte-de-Beaupré: its oldest part was built between 1660 and 1667, and it was enlarged twice. In 1677 the Quebec merchants Charles Aubert de la Chesnaye and Charles Bazire had their land here made the fief of Charleville; it went back to the seigneury of Beaupré in 1694. Classified in 1965.",
  "A long, low stone house of a storey and a half under a steep hipped roof with short eaves, a central stone chimney and windows set off-centre, standing back from the road on large grounds.",
  "",
  "5580 avenue Royale. A private house as far as any source says: look from avenue Royale. Owner not checked.",
  [HP('Manoir de Charleville',4475)],brick='no'),
N('maison-laberge-ange-gardien',"Maison Laberge","L'Ange-Gardien",'qc','landmark','standing',46.9184,-71.0916,'site','view','walk','','1674',
  "Among the oldest houses in Quebec: the first block was built in 1674 for Robert de La Berge, a Norman settler who came in 1658, lengthened in 1692 and again in 1791. Twelve generations of the Laberge family lived in it, from 1674 to 1970. Classified in 1974.",
  "A long, low house of roughcast and whitewashed stone with a steep, flared roof, windows set irregularly and more of them on the sunny south front, and a board-sided lean-to at the northeast. It sits on a rise above avenue Royale looking over the river to Île d'Orléans.",
  "A Norman settler's house of 1674, lived in by one family for 296 years.",
  "24 rue de la Mairie. Owner not checked: look from the street. Lot on rue de la Mairie (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP('Maison Laberge',5026)],th=[46.91834,-71.0908],thn='Lot on rue de la Mairie (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('chapelles-procession-ange-gardien',"Chapelles de procession Notre-Dame-de-Grâce and Saint-Roch","L'Ange-Gardien",'qc','worship','standing',46.9175,-71.0894,'site','view','walk','','before 1753',
  "A pair of procession chapels, both built before 1753, one east and one west of the parish church on avenue Royale, marking the ends of the village. They served as resting altars on processions such as Corpus Christi, practised in Quebec until the mid-20th century. Both were classified in 1981.",
  "Two small roughcast stone chapels alike: a rectangular nave ending in a round apse, a slightly flared roof and a small belfry on the front. Notre-Dame-de-Grâce is at 6357 avenue Royale, east of the church; Saint-Roch is west of the church, about 700 m along the road. Interiors not checked.",
  "",
  "Roadside on avenue Royale; owner not checked. The pin is Notre-Dame-de-Grâce. The town-hall lot on rue de la Mairie is about 150 m away (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP('Chapelle de procession Notre-Dame-de-Grâce',4933),HP('Chapelle de procession Saint-Roch',4934)],th=[46.91834,-71.0908],thn='Lot on rue de la Mairie (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('vieux-couvent-chateau-richer',"Vieux couvent de Château-Richer (Aux Trois Couvents)",'Château-Richer','qc','civic','standing',46.9689,-71.0187,'site','open','walk','','1907',
  "Teaching on this site goes back to a Congregation of Notre-Dame mission of 1689; that convent burned in 1759. A parish school followed in 1830, then the Sisters of the Good Shepherd from 1870, and the present brick convent was built in 1907 for the Sisters of Notre-Dame du Perpétuel Secours. Digs found the remains of the two earlier convents and the foundations of a windmill of about 1653 to 1655. Since 2000 it has housed the Côte-de-Beaupré interpretation centre, now named Aux Trois Couvents.",
  "A three-storey brick building with a mansard roof and dormers, a raised basement and a tall bell-cote over the centre bay. Exhibits on the archaeology and history of the Côte-de-Beaupré.",
  "",
  "7976 avenue Royale, by the church square. Hours not checked: the museum's web address now shows an unrelated gambling page. Lot on rue du Couvent (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP('Vieux couvent de Château-Richer',13234),W("Centre d'Interprétation de la Côte-de-Beaupré")],th=[46.97017,-71.01875],thn='Lot on rue du Couvent (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('place-eglise-chateau-richer',"Place de l'Église and the church of La Visitation",'Château-Richer','qc','worship','standing',46.97016,-71.01816,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','1658; church 1866',
  "A church stood on this promontory over the river from 1658; Olivier Letardif, first seigneur of Beaupré, was buried in its crypt in 1665. The church and the new presbytery were among the few buildings in Château-Richer spared when British soldiers set fires in 1759. The present stone church, by François-Xavier Berlinguet, went up in 1866 on the same spot; the presbytery is of 1833 and the Lourdes grotto of 1930–1931.",
  "The church (single nave, narrower choir, round apse, round-arched windows and an oculus), a cemetery first laid out in the later 1600s with a stone charnel house and a low stone wall, the roughcast presbytery with its covered gallery, the arched stone grotto at the foot of the promontory and a Sacred Heart monument. Interior not checked.",
  "",
  "Rue de l'Église off avenue Royale. Service times not found on a parish page. Lot on rue du Couvent beside the church (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Site du patrimoine de la Place-de-l'Église",13302)],th=[46.97017,-71.01875],thn='Lot on rue du Couvent (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('eglise-saint-joachim',"Église de Saint-Joachim",'Saint-Joachim','qc','worship','standing',47.05461,-70.84377,'exact','view','walk','','1771–1779',
  "A stone church built from 1771 to 1779 to the traditional plan Bishop Briand favoured, with a new eclectic front and tower by David Ouellet in 1895. Its interior, designed by François Baillairgé and his son Thomas and carried out from 1816 to 1829 under the guidance of Abbé Jérôme Demers, is one of the first unified neoclassical church interiors in Quebec. Across the street, the presbytery of 1766, where Mass was said until the church opened about 1779, was lengthened in 1828–1831. Both were classified in 1959.",
  "Inside, by the Register: a gilded coffered false vault, an altarpiece with paired fluted Corinthian pilasters, banded columns topped with incense burners and life-size statues, a pulpit with its stair, the churchwardens' pew back, choir windows of stained glass and a Napoléon Déry organ in the gallery. Outside, the cemetery with its calvary and a Sacred Heart monument, and the long stone presbytery with its wooden doorcases imitating cut stone. Interior hours not checked.",
  "",
  "Rue de l'Église, in the village. Service times not found on a parish page. Lot on rue de l'Église just south (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP('Église de Saint-Joachim',12381),HP('Presbytère de Saint-Joachim',11424)],th=[47.0542,-70.84386],thn="Lot on rue de l'Église (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),
N('la-grande-ferme-saint-joachim',"La Grande Ferme",'Saint-Joachim','qc','landmark','standing',47.05178,-70.81845,'exact','open','walk','','1641; house 1866',
  "Bishop Laval acquired land at Saint-Joachim in 1662 and set up a farm here to supply the Petit Séminaire de Québec; the museum dates the first stone house to 1641 and the first church of Saint-Joachim, on the farm, to 1685. British troops attacked the area on August 23, 1759 and the farm was destroyed. The Séminaire rebuilt it in 1866 as the present long stone farmhouse, recognised in 1975 and a museum since 1979.",
  "The 1866 house: a storey and a half of stone under a flared roof, with a permanent exhibit on daily life at the farm and rooms furnished upstairs. On the grounds, the archaeological remains of the earlier houses, barns, an enclosing wall and the site of the 1685 church, with gardens and sculpture, below Cap Tourmente.",
  "",
  "800 chemin du Cap-Tourmente. Run as a museum (hours from its own page); café and shop. Guided tours Tuesday to Sunday at 11 a.m. and 2 p.m. Lot on chemin du Cap-Tourmente beside the house (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP('La Grande-Ferme',1718),L('Musée de La Grande Ferme','https://lagrandeferme.org/')],th=[47.05179,-70.81769],thn='Lot on chemin du Cap-Tourmente (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Mondays'),
]
